---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Enso from '../components/Enso.astro';
import Seal from '../components/Seal.astro';
import { HYPOTHESES } from '../data/hypotheses';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const ESSAY = 'https://ejhong.substack.com/p/knots-of-existence-hypotheses';
const ORIGINAL = 'https://ejhong.substack.com/p/knots-of-existence-and-vasocomputation';
---
<Base current="intro">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-canvas" aria-hidden="true">
      <canvas id="hero-canvas"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-text">
      <p class="eyebrow">An atlas of the body’s held places</p>
      <h1>Knots</h1>
      <p class="lede">Press into one, and breathe out slowly. Something lets go in seconds.</p>
      <p class="intro">
        Anyone who has lain on a foam roller has met them. Medicine has argued for a century about whether they exist.
        This is a visual introduction to one answer — that a knot is a staple in the fascia, held stuck — to its rivals,
        and to the maps the contemplative traditions drew of the same ground.
      </p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn accent" href={`${base}/atlas/`}>Enter the atlas</a>
        <a class="btn" href="#observation">Read on</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="hero-note mono lower">Every point of light is one of ~100,000 perforators. The red ones are stuck.</p>
  </section>

  <article class="chapters">
    <section id="observation" class="chapter">
      <aside class="glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">息</aside>
      <p class="num-label mono">01 · The observation</p>
      <h2>A change of state, not of structure</h2>
      <p>
        The whole subject rests on one observation available to anyone: <em>pressure locates a knot, and a slow exhale releases it, in seconds.</em>
        Tissue does not remodel in five seconds. Collagen, scar and adhesion change over days to weeks. Whatever lets go mid-exhale must be a
        <strong>state</strong> — something the body is actively doing, and can stop doing quickly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Knots accumulate with age. They cluster at the junctions — the base of the skull, the neck, the shoulder blades, the low back, the hips — and
        in the brace muscles of jaw, throat and belly. They answer to breath. And the people who take release work furthest report something the
        trigger-point literature does not describe: the knots go with a small pop, felt as a spark or a <em>star</em>; they release along paths, and the
        paths converge on the back of the skull, where released knots are replaced by others, <em>as if from a queue</em>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter">
      <aside class="glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">二</aside>
      <p class="num-label mono">02 · Two families</p>
      <h2>Knots first, then the sheet</h2>
      <p>
        Go further with the same work — more hours, more breath, more attention, over months — and a second set of reports appears. Layers
        separate, strand by strand, with a crackle. A layer at the back of the head peels and spreads forward over the face. Regions feel hollowed
        and unsupported. Something air-like moves through connected pockets and vents at the back of the mouth. Long chains slide back into place
        over days. The hollows fill, as if stitched.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read as one story, the reports are baffling. Read as two, they organise themselves. The <strong>knots</strong> respond to pressure and breath
        in seconds. The <strong>sheet</strong> — the separating, hollowing, filling and chains — unfolds over hours and days and does not respond to
        rolling at all. And the order is strict: the knots go first, and the separating cannot begin at a site until its knots are gone. Two
        timescales, two triggers, a strict order: two systems, meeting at one anatomical fact.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter wide">
      <aside class="glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">結</aside>
      <p class="num-label mono">03 · The staple</p>
      <h2>Where something passes through</h2>
      <p class="measure">
        The superficial fascia — the layer under the skin, above the muscle — is fixed to the layer beneath in two ways. There are passive fibrous
        septa. And there are the places where something passes through: a small artery with its veins, a cutaneous nerve and a lymphatic, rising
        together through a fibrous ring. Anatomists call it the perforating triad; surgeons call the sites <em>perforators</em>, and find them with a
        pencil Doppler before raising a flap, because a flap will not lift until the perforators tethering it are dealt with.
      </p>
      <p class="measure">Suppose the knots are these staples, held stuck. Each part of the bundle then explains one thing reported about a knot.</p>
      <div class="triad">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">The artery</p>
          <h3>is the smooth muscle</h3>
          <p>
            Skin arterioles are the most sympathetically governed vessels in the body. A deep inhale constricts them within about two seconds — an
            autonomic test for decades — and a slow exhale lets them open. Release on the out-breath is simply what this vessel does.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">The nerve</p>
          <h3>is the star</h3>
          <p>
            A constricted perforator starves its own nerve. When the vessel opens, the nerve reperfuses and its patch of skin lights up — a foot
            waking up, miniaturised to one territory. The sensation belongs to the nerve, not the muscle.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">The collar</p>
          <h3>is the order</h3>
          <p>
            Hyaluronan around the bundle gels when the tissue turns acidic and cool, and melts with warmth, washout and shear. A shut vessel makes its
            own collar. Open it and the collar melts; only then can the sheet slide. The reported order is a mechanical requirement.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter">
      <aside class="glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">根</aside>
      <p class="num-label mono">04 · Trees and roots</p>
      <h2>Why the knots go to the ridge</h2>
      <p>
        Perforators do not move. But every perforator belongs to a tree, and every tree has a root. Arterioles <em>conduct</em>: a dilation that
        starts at a branch propagates along the vessel wall toward its feeding vessel at millimetres to centimetres a second (Segal &amp; Duling, 1986).
        And a downstream vessel cannot stay open while the trunk that feeds it is shut — which is why cleared areas come back until something upstream
        clears too.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the felt release climbs the tree and queues at the trunk’s entry. On the back of the head that entry is a real gate: the occipital artery
        emerges through the deep fascia between trapezius and sternocleidomastoid at the superior nuchal line, a few centimetres from the midline, with
        the greater occipital nerve beside it. Every perforator of the posterior scalp is downstream of it. In the atlas, releases send light up their
        trees toward these roots.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter">
      <aside class="glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">膜</aside>
      <p class="num-label mono">05 · The sheet</p>
      <h2>One sheet, built as rooms with doors</h2>
      <p>
        The scalp and face are covered by one continuous sheet — the galea aponeurotica, becoming the temporoparietal fascia, becoming the SMAS of the
        face — over a plane of loose tissue that anatomists call the danger area of the scalp, because anything introduced into it spreads over the
        whole cranium. Below the neck the same arrangement continues over the entire body, pinned to bone along particular lines: the nuchal line,
        mastoid, cheekbone, jaw, collarbone, the iliac crest and inguinal ligament.
      </p>
      <p>
        On this reading the second family is the sheet behaving once its staples are free. Separation is septa giving way, one at a time. The chains
        are folds propagating along a loosened membrane — nothing is pulled; a fold advances. The gates are attachment lines where folds pile up.
        Hollowing is a freed pocket. Filling is re-adhesion — fibrin strands bridging within days, collagen within two weeks — literally strand by
        strand, which is why it is felt as stitching.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter wide">
      <aside class="glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">数</aside>
      <p class="num-label mono">06 · A ladder of a hundred thousand</p>
      <h2>What a branching tree looks like from the skin</h2>
      <p class="measure">
        Practitioners who count report a hierarchy: a handful of roots at the ridges, big knots that take sessions to clear, and micro-knots at dozens
        per square inch — hundreds of thousands in a body, by their own estimate. That is the vascular ladder.
      </p>
      <div class="ladder">
        <div><span class="big num">~40</span><span class="mono lower">source arteries · angiosomes</span><span class="desc">Taylor &amp; Palmer’s vascular territories; the roots.</span></div>
        <div><span class="big num">374</span><span class="mono lower">major perforators</span><span class="desc">Average count of perforators ≥ 0.5 mm plotted per subject (1987).</span></div>
        <div><span class="big num">~4,000</span><span class="mono lower">medium perforators</span><span class="desc">The branching middle, where most big knots would sit.</span></div>
        <div><span class="big num">~100,000</span><span class="mono lower">small perforators</span><span class="desc">One every 4–5 mm across ~1.8 m² of skin: the micro-knots.</span></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter">
      <aside class="glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">齢</aside>
      <p class="num-label mono">07 · Across a life</p>
      <h2>Children are nearly clean</h2>
      <p>
        Knots accumulate with age; the young have few. Cutaneous microvascular function declines with age and resting sympathetic tone rises. The two
        curves have never been laid on top of each other, and on this hypothesis they should match site by site. In the atlas, drag the age from one
        to ninety — or play a life — and watch the ladder fill from the roots down.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter">
      <aside class="glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">枕</aside>
      <p class="num-label mono">08 · The knot at the base of the brain</p>
      <h2>The master tether</h2>
      <p>
        Accounts of awakening, across traditions, often place the decisive moment low at the back of the head, where the brain meets the neck. The
        anatomy there is a ring. Behind, the nuchal ligament anchors the whole posterior sheet and the two occipital trees meet. Beneath, in the
        suboccipital triangle, the densest muscle spindles in the body report where the head is — and a band of tissue discovered in 1995, the
        myodural bridge, ties those muscles to the dura around the brainstem. In front, the throat’s constrictors hang from the base of the skull.
      </p>
      <p>
        A release here would change everything at once. The traditions put it here too: Fengfu, the Wind Palace, at the midline seam; Naohu, the
        Brain’s Door, on the bump; and for the Daoist alchemists the whole region was the <em>Jade Pillow</em> — the third and hardest gate.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter">
      <aside class="glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">経</aside>
      <p class="num-label mono">09 · What the traditions knew</p>
      <h2>There are knots; breath is the tool; untying them changes consciousness</h2>
      <p>
        Nearly every tradition that mapped the inner body arrived at those three claims. Tibetan tsa lung describes channel-knots untied by working
        the winds with breath; yoga names three granthis along the central channel; the kuṇḍalinī literature describes energy travelling and
        involuntary movement. Chinese medicine turns out to have described <em>both</em> systems: tender <em>ashi</em> points and acupoints — about
        eighty percent of which sit where a neurovascular bundle perforates the superficial fascia (Heine, 1988) — and the sinew channels, whose
        defining feature is that each one <em>knots</em> (結, jié) at bony prominences: the attachment lines of the sheet.
      </p>
      <p>
        Correspondence found after the fact is the weakest kind of evidence. But it is now specific enough to be coded blind and tested. The atlas
        overlays these maps on the same body so they can be compared.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter wide">
      <aside class="glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">説</aside>
      <p class="num-label mono">10 · Rival answers</p>
      <h2>What else a knot could be</h2>
      <div class="rivals">
        {
          HYPOTHESES.map((h) => (
            <a class="rival" href={`${base}/hypotheses/#${h.id}`}>
              <span class="dot" style={`--c:${h.color}`} />
              <span class="r-name">{h.name}</span>
              <span class="r-who mono lower">{h.who}</span>
              <span class="r-short">{h.short}</span>
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter">
      <aside class="glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">験</aside>
      <p class="num-label mono">11 · What would show it</p>
      <h2>An afternoon with a Doppler pen</h2>
      <ol class="tests">
        <li><strong>Map the staples.</strong> One blinded examiner marks tender points; another maps perforators with a pencil Doppler; a third computes coincidence against chance.</li>
        <li><strong>Film a star.</strong> Laser speckle over a knot and a sham site: a local perfusion flash on the exhale, time-locked to the felt release — and absent during an inhale-hold.</li>
        <li><strong>Free a collar without breath.</strong> Hydrodissect a perforator under ultrasound. If the knot is a collared perforator, it lets go with the fluid.</li>
        <li><strong>Time the chains.</strong> Does a travelling sensation ride a travelling perfusion wave at the speed of conducted vasodilation?</li>
        <li><strong>Lay the curves together.</strong> Knot burden and cutaneous microvascular reactivity, site by site, across the life span.</li>
        <li><strong>Image the seam.</strong> Before and after a base-of-skull release: suboccipital muscles, dural displacement, cervical rotation.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter caution">
      <aside class="glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">慎</aside>
      <p class="num-label mono">12 · A caution</p>
      <h2>The gate is anatomy, not a target</h2>
      <p>
        Everything that admits air into the body, separates a plane or stretches a nerve is an injury, and the practices that would do it are
        documented hazards. The suboccipital triangle sits over the vertebral artery; deep pressure there is a known stroke risk. Forceful blowing,
        breath-holds and straining cause emphysema of the face and chest. The traditions preserved a warning about untying too fast; the modern
        literature on intensive practice records destabilisation lasting months to years. Nothing here is a protocol. The benefits are slow; the
        dangers concentrate in speed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter coda">
      <Enso size={120} seed={21} class="coda-enso" />
      <p class="lede">Someone should put a Doppler pen there and press.</p>
      <p class="mono lower">— the last line of <a href={ESSAY}>Knots of Existence Hypotheses</a> (2026), from which this site is drawn. See also <a href={ORIGINAL}>Knots of Existence and Vasocomputation</a>.</p>
      <div class="cta center">
        <a class="btn accent" href={`${base}/atlas/`}>Enter the atlas</a>
        <a class="btn" href={`${base}/library/`}>The library</a>
      </div>
      <Seal size={30} class="coda-seal" />
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<script>
  import { mountHero } from '../viewer/ui/HeroScene';
  const c = document.getElementById('hero-canvas') as HTMLCanvasElement;
  mountHero(c);
</script>

<style>
  .hero {
    position: relative;
    min-height: calc(100dvh - 4rem);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0 var(--gutter);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .hero-canvas {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }
  .hero-canvas canvas {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity 2.4s var(--ease);
  }
  .hero-canvas :global(canvas.ready) {
    opacity: 1;
  }
  .hero::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: auto 0 0 0;
    height: 22%;
    background: linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent, var(--bg));
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: 1;
  }
  .hero-text {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
    max-width: 34rem;
    padding: 4rem 0 6rem;
  }
  .hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(5rem, 13vw, 11rem);
    font-weight: 300;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 0.9;
    margin: 1.2rem 0 1.6rem -0.05em;
  }
  .lede {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 2.6vw, 2.2rem);
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin: 0 0 1.4rem;
  }
  .intro {
    color: var(--ink-2);
    max-width: 30rem;
  }
  .cta {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.8rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .cta a {
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .cta.center {
    justify-content: center;
  }
  .hero-note {
    z-index: 2;
    position: absolute;
    right: var(--gutter);
    bottom: 1.6rem;
    font-size: 0.66rem;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .chapters {
    padding: 0 var(--gutter);
    counter-reset: ch;
  }
  .chapter {
    position: relative;
    max-width: 40rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 9rem 0 0;
  }
  .chapter.wide {
    max-width: 68rem;
  }
  .chapter .measure {
    max-width: 40rem;
  }
  .chapter h2 {
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 4.2vw, 3.4rem);
    margin: 0.9rem 0 1.8rem;
  }
  .chapter h3 {
    font-size: 1.55rem;
    margin: 0.3rem 0 0.8rem;
  }
  .num-label {
    color: var(--ink-3);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .glyph {
    position: absolute;
    left: -9rem;
    top: 8.4rem;
    font-size: 4.2rem;
    color: var(--ink-4);
    opacity: 0.55;
    line-height: 1;
    writing-mode: vertical-rl;
    user-select: none;
  }
  .chapter.wide .glyph {
    left: -6rem;
  }
  .chapter p {
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .chapter strong {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .chapter em {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .triad {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 2.5rem;
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 2.2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hair);
  }
  .triad p:last-child {
    font-size: 1.02rem;
  }

  .ladder {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 2rem;
    margin-top: 3rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hair);
    padding-top: 2.2rem;
  }
  .ladder div {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    align-content: start;
  }
  .ladder .big {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-feature-settings: 'lnum';
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 4.6vw, 4.2rem);
    font-weight: 300;
    color: var(--ink);
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .ladder div:last-child .big {
    color: var(--shu);
  }
  .ladder .mono {
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .ladder .desc {
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .rivals {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(18rem, 1fr));
    gap: 0;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hair);
  }
  .rival {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 0.9rem 1fr;
    gap: 0.2rem 0.7rem;
    padding: 1.4rem 1.2rem 1.5rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hair);
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
  }
  .rival .dot {
    width: 7px;
    height: 7px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--c);
    margin-top: 0.62rem;
  }
  .r-name {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.45rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }
  .r-who {
    grid-column: 2;
    font-size: 0.66rem;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .r-short {
    grid-column: 2;
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
  }
  .rival:hover .r-name {
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--c);
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }

  .tests {
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .tests li {
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
    padding-left: 0.4rem;
  }
  .tests li::marker {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }

  .caution h2 {
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .coda {
    text-align: center;
    display: grid;
    justify-items: center;
    gap: 1.2rem;
    padding-top: 10rem;
  }
  .coda :global(.coda-enso) {
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .coda .lede {
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3.4vw, 2.8rem);
    max-width: 30rem;
  }
  .coda :global(.coda-seal) {
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 1180px) {
    .glyph {
      display: none;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .hero {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      align-items: end;
      min-height: 100dvh;
    }
    .hero-canvas {
      inset: 0;
      opacity: 0.75;
    }
    .hero-text {
      padding-bottom: 5rem;
    }
    .triad,
    .ladder {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .triad,
    .ladder {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .chapter {
      padding-top: 6rem;
    }
  }
</style>
